WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.851
Blood pressure is the push of the blood on the walls of the vessels. We measure it in millimetres of mercury, written m m H g. The ventricles create this pressure when they contract.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:35.662
In an artery, the pressure goes up and down with each beat. The highest pressure is the systolic pressure, when the ventricles contract. The lowest is the diastolic pressure, when the ventricles relax. For example, a typical reading is about one hundred and twenty over eighty. These are example numbers, not real data from one person.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:50.332
Now a whole journey. Region one is the left ventricle. Its pressure swings from about one hundred and twenty right down to nearly zero. Only a heart chamber falls almost to zero.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:01:03.013
In the arteries the pressure stays high, and it still rises and falls with each beat. This is the pulse. Further from the heart, in the arterioles, the small arteries, the swings get smaller.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:16.300
In the capillaries the pressure falls smoothly, with no pulse. Capillary walls have no muscle or elastic tissue. The pressure falls because they are far from the heart, they are narrow, and fluid leaks out.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:27.183
In the veins the pressure is lowest. Blood always flows from higher pressure to lower pressure. That is why it moves from arteries, through capillaries, into veins.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:44.693
So in a graph question, match each region to a vessel. Swings to nearly zero mean the ventricle. High swings mean arteries. A smooth fall means capillaries. The lowest pressure means veins.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:59.401
Here is a real exam question. The graph shows blood pressure in five regions of the circulation. Region one goes up to about one hundred and twenty, then down almost to zero, again and again.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:08.195
The question asks: explain why blood pressure varies in region one. It is worth five marks, so plan six ideas.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:22.447
Idea one: it falls almost to zero, so region one is the heart, the left ventricle. Idea two: the ventricle muscle contracts. Idea three: this forces blood out into the aorta, so the pressure is high.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:34.996
Idea four: then the ventricle relaxes. Idea five: so the pressure falls to nearly zero. Idea six: this happens with every heartbeat, so it rises and falls again and again.

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:41.236
Here is the full answer. This explanation is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:53.958
A common mistake is to say the atria make the high pressure. It is the ventricles. Their thick muscle forces blood into the arteries.

00:02:54.550 --> 00:03:04.543
Another mistake is to mix up the words. Systolic is the highest, when the ventricles contract. Diastolic is the lowest, when they relax.

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:25.432
Your turn. A nurse measures a patient's blood pressure as one hundred and twenty-five over eighty. Which number is the systolic pressure, and what causes it? Then explain why blood flows from an artery to a vein through a dialysis machine, which cleans the blood when the kidneys fail. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:39.666
One hundred and twenty-five is the systolic pressure, the highest. It happens when the ventricles contract and force blood into the arteries. Eighty is the diastolic pressure, when they relax.

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:51.801
Blood in the artery is at high pressure. Blood in the vein is at low pressure. Blood always flows from high to low pressure, so it flows from the artery, through the machine, into the vein.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:04:05.491
Every time: match each region to a vessel. Link a rise to the ventricles contracting, and a fall to them relaxing. And remember: blood flows from high pressure to low.
